; A `parallel` binding that traps inside a recovery point is answered at
; the ARMING CALL, once, with the trap's status (MM-PAR-7).
;
; Until 2026-09-27 the forked child inherited the parent's recovery
; point with the rest of its memory. A binding that divided by zero
; inside `__axiom_recover` therefore jumped to the PARENT's arm site in
; the CHILD, which ran the parent's continuation - this program printed
; `after recover: status 72` from the child - and exited 0, which the
; parent's join read as success with the answer 0: the parent printed
; `after recover: status 1`. Two lines where one belongs, the second of
; them wrong, and no error visible to either process.
;
; The child now starts disarmed, with an empty registry of its own; its
; trap is an exit with status 72, and the join re-raises that status
; THROUGH the parent's recovery point, which answers it. The child's
; sentence is on fd 2 (`.err`); the parent's stdout carries one line.
;
; The default lowering is what this case measures. Under `--threads` a
; binding's trap ends the process, as `docs/reference.md`'s `parallel`
; section states; `471-parallel-trap.ax` is the case both lowerings run.
(import IO)
(import Str)

(:: zero Int)
(fn (zero)
  0)

(:: boom (-> Int Int))
(fn (boom x)
  (/ 10 zero))

(:: body (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (body x)
  (parallel p (
    (a (+ x 1))
    (b (boom x))
  )
    (+ a b)))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (main)
  (let ((st 
    (__axiom_recover
      __axiom_arena_mark
      (lambda (x) (body x)))))
    {
      (println "after recover: status {st}")
      0
    }))
